# Set up the environment
Create a folder named "tocopy" on your machine, at /home/$USER/. If you want to use a different name or path, don't forget to adapt the Makefile's "copy" rule's variables.

Prepare a .env file inside.

Example of .env:
````
POSTGRES_USER=insertUSERhere
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=insertPASSWORDhere
POSTGRES_DB=nameyourDB
SESSION_SECRET=chooseAsessionSecretOfAtLeast32Chars
````

CAREFUL: once the username and password have been used to create the database, you won't be able to change them without erasing all its data.

# Make commands
## Build and Launch the stack
To build and launch the stack, type:
```
make
```

## Stop the stack
```
make down
```

## Restart the stack
```
make up
```

## Stop and restart the stack
```
make re
```

## Stop and delete the stack
**!! CAREFUL !!

This action will erase ALL DATA.**

```
make fclean
```

# Secrets management
Secrets are managed by HarshiCorp Vault.

During the building phase, Vault prepares AppRoles for the services that need to retrieve their respective secrets, and stores the related ids and keys in a shared volume named "certs_data".

Vault generates a certificate for NGINX, and stores it as secret in the volume "vault_data" (encrypted and isolated). Only with the id and key found in the shared volume will NGINX be allowed to retrieve this certificate.

Vault takes the environment variables and stores them as secrets in the volume "vault_data" (encrypted and isolated). Only with the id and key from the shared volume will the backend be allowed to retrieve these environment variables.

All temporary files that are used in order for this to work are erased as soon as they served their purpose. So is the global .env.

That way, every secret is written only in RAM, in isolated volumes, and is kept encrypted. Only the services that need them have access to them. 

For NGINX, the retrieval of the certificate happens through its script: it then stores it in files isolated inside the NGINX docker, so that the configuration file can access them.

For the backend, a function was created in the file vault.ts: its only purpose is to use the backend's id and key to retrieve the secret it needs, "DATABASE_URL". The file prisma.service.ts awaits for it to be done so that it can use the url for its connection to the database.